.TH MKDRAW 1 "November 2026" "MK BBS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mkdraw \- text mode screen editor for BBS screens (ANSI, AVATAR, ASCII, Pascal source)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkdraw
[\fB\-n\fR] [\fIFILE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkdraw
draws and edits the 80 x 23 text screens that bulletin board systems show to their callers: ANSI art, AVATAR screens, plain ASCII
screens and the Pascal source that writes a screen.
Every cell of the screen is one IBM PC (CP437) character and one colour attribute (a 4 bit foreground, a 3 bit background and
blink), as on a DOS machine.
It is the Free Pascal conversion of the DOS program MKDRAW and uses the Free Vision text mode interface.
.PP
The terminal must be at least 80 columns by 25 rows.
A UTF-8 terminal shows the Unicode equivalents of the CP437 characters, and what is typed or pasted there is turned back into CP437 codes.
Files are read and written as BBS software has them: CP437 bytes with CR LF line ends.
.PP
With a \fIFILE\fR that file is loaded (a file that is not there is an empty screen with that name).
Without one the screen files (\fI*.a*\fR) of the current directory are listed to choose from; Esc starts with an empty screen.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Leave the files as the original DOS program wrote them.
Without this option a saved ANSI file (and the Pascal source) is made so that every colour reset (ESC[0m, ESC[m) also sets the
foreground and the background (light gray on black, the DOS default), so that a terminal with its own default colours shows the art
as it was drawn.
.SH KEYS
Esc opens the fast menu with every command.
Alt-H shows the help.
.TP
.B Alt-A
choose the colour and attribute
.TP
.B Alt-B, Alt-I
blinking, intensity
.TP
.B Alt-U
take the colour under the cursor
.TP
.B Alt-R
reverse the attribute
.TP
.B Alt-D
drawing mode (the arrow keys draw with a line set)
.TP
.B Alt-C, Alt-M
copy, move an area
.TP
.B Alt-F
fill an area with the attribute
.TP
.B Alt-Z
zap (clear) an area
.TP
.B Alt-E
enclose an area in a frame
.TP
.B Alt-V, Alt-Y
insert, delete a line
.TP
.B Alt-W
double image (2 x 2)
.TP
.B Alt-G
choose a character set of special characters
.TP
.B F1..F10
enter the special characters of the current set (Alt-F1..F10 chooses set 1..10, Ctrl-F1..F5 set 11..15, Alt-1..Alt-9 set 1..9)
.TP
.B Ctrl-A..Ctrl-Z
enter that control code
.TP
.B Alt-P
pick a BBS screen code (see FILES)
.TP
.B Alt-L
load a screen
.TP
.B Alt-S, Alt-T
save an area, save the whole screen
.TP
.B Alt-X
leave
.PP
An area is chosen with the arrow keys and Enter (or Space) for each corner; L takes the last area again.
The mouse of a terminal moves the cursor, and the right button is Esc.
A name without an extension saves the screen as \fIname.asc\fR, \fIname.ans\fR and \fIname.avt\fR; a name with the extension \fI.ans\fR or \fI.avt\fR saves that kind only, \fI.pas\fR or \fI.inc\fR the Pascal source that writes the screen, and any other extension plain ASCII.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
the terminal type
.TP
.B MK
the folder of the BBS, where
.I codes.mkd
is looked for second
.TP
.B HOME
where the user's own
.I codes.mkd
is looked for
.SH FILES
.TP
.I codes.mkd
The list of BBS screen codes that Alt-P offers (the codes a BBS expands when it shows a screen).
It has two lines for each code: the text shown in the list and the code itself.
The first of these is used: \fIcodes.mkd\fR in the current directory,
in the folder named by \fBMK\fR,
\fI~/.config/mkdraw/codes.mkd\fR,
\fI/usr/local/share/mkdraw/codes.mkd\fR,
\fI/usr/share/mkdraw/codes.mkd\fR.
Without any of them a built-in list is used.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
done
.TP
.B 2
the terminal is smaller than 80 x 25
.TP
.B 3
a wrong command line
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkcat (1)
.PP
The MK BBS documentation (\fI/usr/share/doc/mkbbs\fR or \fI/usr/share/doc/mkdraw\fR).
